1 on **b6**? No — wait, **PBF1** is listed on **g7** and **f7** — that must be a typo in interpretation.\n\nWait — rechecking:  \nLooking at rank 6: "PBF1" on **b6** and **e6** — yes! So black played **...b7-b6?** and **...e7-e6?** — but **PBF1** means “moved two squares last turn” — but **b6** and **e6** are only one square from starting rank. Contradiction.\n\nAh — correction: **PBF1** means the pawn **did move two squares last turn**, so only pawns on **rank 4 (for white)** or **rank 5 (for black)** can have T flag. But here, **PBF1** appears on **b6** and **e6** — which are **rank 6**, so cannot be two-square movers. This suggests the **2Step flag may be mislabeled or incorrectly applied**.\n\nBut per encoding: `2Step` = `T` if moved 2 squares *last turn*. So only pawns on **rank 4 (white)** or **rank 5 (black)** can have `T`. Here, **PBF1** on b6 and e6 — both on rank 6 — implies they moved one square — so should be `F`, not `T`. Likely, the `1` in `PBF1` is actually the **Moved** flag, not the 2Step. Ah — the format is `[Type][Color][2Step][Moved]`.\n\nSo:\n- `PBF1` → this would mean: Pawn, Black, 2Step=?, Moved=1\nBut where is the 2Step value?\n\nWait — the example says: `PWF1`: White Pawn, NOT en passant eligible, already moved.\n\nSo format: `[Type][Color][2Step][Moved]`\n\nTherefore:\n- `PBF1` → Type=P, Color=B, 2Step=F (not eligible for en passant), Moved=1 (has moved)\n- `PBF0` → Moved=0 (never moved)\n\nSo the `1` or `0` at the end is the **Moved** flag.\n\nThen where is the 2Step flag? It\'s the third character: F or T.\n\nSo only pawns that moved **two squares last move** have `T` in the third position.\n\nNow check: any pawn with `T` in third position?\n\nLook:  \n- `PBF1` — third char is `1` → that\'s the **Moved** flag.  \nWait — inconsistency!\n\nExamples given:\n- `PWF1`: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved → so format: P W F(2Step) 1(Moved)\n- `RBF0`: R B F(2Step) 0(Moved)\n\nSo the **third character is 2Step (F/T)**, **fourt