  * `P` - Pawn\n        * Color (Player):\n            * `W` - White (moves first)\n            * `B` - Black\n        * 2Step (En Passant flag)\n            * `F` - Did NOT move 2 squares last turn\n            * `T` - Moved 2 squares last turn (en passant eligible)\n        * Moved (Movement flag)\n            * `1` - Has moved before\n            * `0` - Has NOT moved yet\n        \n        ### Examples: \n            * PWF1: White Pawn, not available for en passant, already moved; \n            * RBF0：Black Rook, never moved (castling eligible);\n            * KWT1: White king, irrelevant 2-step flag, has moved;\n            * QWF0 = White queen, irrelevant 2-step flag, never moved.\n\n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly give a overview of the situation, evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output one of the valid actions with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Situation: [Your Observation]\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n        ', 'user_prompt': 'You are playing as White.\nCurrent state representation is as follows:\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n8 |RBF0|    |    |QBF0|KBF0|BBF1|NBF1|    | 8\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n7 |PBF0|    |PBF0|PBF0|NWF1|    |    |PBF0| 7\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n6 |BBF1|PBF1|    |    |PBF1|    |RBF1|    | 6\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n5 |    |    |    |PWF1|    |    |PBF1|    | 5\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n4 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 4\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n3 |    |    |NWF1|    |    |    |    |    | 3\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n2 |PWF0|PWF0|PWF0|    |QWF1|PWF0|PWF0|PWF0| 2\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n1 |RWF0|    |BWF0|    |KWF0|BWF0|    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, N: c3-a4, N: c3-b1, N: c3-b5, N: c3-d1, N: c3-e4, P: d5-e6, P: d5-d6, K: e1-d1, K: e1-d2, Q: e2-d1, Q: e2-d2, Q: e2-d3, Q: e2-c4, Q: e2-b5, Q: e2-a6, Q: e2-e3, Q: e2-e4, Q: e2-e5, Q: e2-e6, Q: e2-f3, Q: e2-g4, Q: e2-h5, N: e7-c6, N: e7-c8, N: e7-f5, N: e7-g6, N: e7-g8, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in a semi-open position with some tension in the center. My king is still on e1, and the castling rights are intact (KWF0), though there are pieces blocking queenside castling. Black has developed their knight to e7 and is potentially preparing to reposition it, while several of their pawns have advanced. My queen on e2 is somewhat exposed but active, and the pawn on d5 presents a tactical opportunity. The black king remains in the center (e8), and there’s potential to open lines against it.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: d5-e6 – This pawn push captures en passant style (though not actually en passant) and opens the center. It attacks the knight on f5 if it were there, but currently just advances with tempo, possibly opening lines for my queen or bishop.\n- Q: e2-h5 – A strong attacking idea; this targets h7, a known weakness in many positions, especially since Black hasn’t castled visibly and h7 is only defended by the king. This could lead to mating threats.\n- N: c3-e4 – Centralizes the knight, increasing pressure in the center and eyeing f6 or d6.\n- P: d5-d6 – A quiet but dangerous pawn push; if accepted by Black capturing (cxd6 or exd6), it opens the e-file for my rook or queen.\n- B: c1-g5 – Develops the bishop actively, pinning a potential knight on f6, though currently no such target exists.\n- Q: e2-a6 – Targets the weak a7-pawn, but may be premature.\n\nThe most aggressive and promising move is **Q: e2-h5**, threatening Qxh7+ which would force Kxh7 and allow Ng5+ for a quick attack. Even if Black plays g6 to prevent that, the queen is well placed to support central action or transfer to kingside.\n\nHowever, upon closer inspection: h7 is defended by the Black king only *if* the king hasn't moved — but we don’t know its exact status. Still, **Q: e2-h5** creates immediate threats and forces Black to react.\n\nBut wait — **P: d5-e6** is also very strong. If Black recaptures exd6 or even fxe6, it opens up the e-file and diagonals for the bishop and queen. However, d5-e6 is a pawn capture forward? Wait — d5 is occupied by PWF1 (my pawn). But where is the enemy pawn? There's a black pawn on f7 and e7 is empty. No black pawn on e5 — so d5-e6 is just a standard pawn advance, not a capture.\n\nWait — valid move list includes **P: d5-e6** and **P: d5-d6** — both are possible non-capturing moves. Advancing to e6 puts the pawn on e6, which supports f7 ideas and may prepare a sacrifice or restrict Black’s pieces.\n\nBut more compelling is **N: e7-c6** — wait, that’s a Black knight move! No — the valid moves listed are from White’s perspective. So all listed moves are for me (White).\n\nWait — **N: e7-c6**? That square has a white knight? No — e7 has a white knight? Looking back:\n\nRow 7: |PBF0|    |PBF0|PBF0|NWF1|    |    |PBF0| → yes! There is a **white knight on e7** (NWF1)! That’s mine!\n\nThat changes everything.\n\nI have a knight on e7 — deep in enemy territory! And it has valid moves: N: e7-c6, N: e7-c8, N: e7-f5, N: e7-g6, N: e7-g8\n\nFrom e7, jumping to g8 would deliver check: Nf5-g8 is not, but e7-g8? Knight from e7 to g8 — that’s two up, one right? e→f→g, 7→8? No — knight moves 2 in one direction, 1 perpendicular. From e7:\n\n- 2 up = e9 invalid\n- 2 down = e5 + left/right → c6, d5? Already covered\n- 2 left = c7 + up/down → c8, c6\n- 2 right = g7 + up/down → g8, g6\n- So yes: **N: e7-g8** is a valid move — and it attacks the Black king directly if it’s still on e8!\n\nIs the Black king on e8? Yes — KBF0 is on e8.\n\nSo **N: e7-g8** delivers check! Because knight from e7 to g8 is L-shaped: 2 files right (e→g), 1 rank up (7→8)? No — 7 to 8 is up one, e to g is two — yes! That’s a knight move.\n\nAnd g8 is currently empty (no piece shown), so **N: e7-g8+** checks the Black king!\n\nEven better — if Black king is on e8, then knight on g8 attacks f6, h6, etc., but more importantly, **it forks the king and possibly the rook on h8**?\n\nAfter Nxe7-g8+, Black must move the king — likely to f8 or e7. But then I may follow up with Qh5 or other attacks.\n\nBut is it safe? The knight on e7 — was it protected before? It came from somewhere — now it's isolated, but moving to g8 is a check, so it cannot be captured immediately unless defended.\n\nBut after Nxg8, if Black doesn't recapture immediately, I might even threaten Rh1-h8# next if the knight draws the king away.\n\nBut let’s confirm: **N: e7-g8** is in the valid move list — yes.\n\nThis is a **check**, highly forcing, and potentially winning material or leading to mate.\n\nCompare to other moves — developing queen or pushing pawns are slower.\n\nThis knight move is unexpected and powerful.\n\nReason: **N: e7-g8** delivers a check, disrupts Black’s coordination, and threatens further damage. It maximizes initiative and exploits the exposed Black king. It’s rare to have a knight drop into g8 with check — this must be played immediately.\n\nAnswer: N: e7-g8"}
