knight attacks d8? f7 to d8: 1 up, 2 left → yes! d8 is attacked.\n\nSo after Nf7+, black king on e8 must go to:\n- d8 — attacked by knight on f7 → invalid\n- f8 — not attacked by knight? f7 to f8 is adjacent — not a knight move → safe\n- d7 — not attacked? f7 to d7: 2 left, 0 down — no; 1 down, 2 left → f7-e5, etc. No — d7 not attacked → possibly safe\n\nSo king can go to f8 or d7.\n\nBut then I can follow up with other attacks.\n\nEven better: **after Nf7+, if king goes to f8, I can play Qh5+ or something?** But I don’t have a queen developed.\n\nAlternatively, **rook lift**: Rf1-e1, preparing Re1-e3-g3 or something.\n\nBut back to the knight: **N: d8-f7** is a strong move because it:\n\n- Develops a piece deeper into enemy territory\n- Threatens immediate check\n- Forces black to weaken or expose king\n- May lead to winning material or mating attack\n\nCompare to other moves:\n\n- **B: f4-e5** — gains space, pressures d6 and c7\n- **B: f4-d6** — targets c7 and b8, eyes a8-rook\n- **B: f4-c7** — forks a8-rook and e8-king? No — bishop on c7 attacks e5, f4, g3, h2 — not a8 or e8 directly. From c7, a8 is diagonal — yes: c7-b6-a5? No, c7 to a8 is c7-b8-a8? Not diagonal. c7-a8 is two left, one up — not diagonal. Diagonals from c7: a5, b6, d8, e5, f4, g3, h2 — so **d8** is attacked — but d8 had our own knight! So if we play B: f4-c7, we attack d8 — where our knight is! So we’d be threatening our own piece — bad.\n\nThus **B: f4-c7** is dangerous unless knight moves first.\n\nTherefore, better to move knight first.\n\nNow, between **N: d8-f7** and **N: d8-e6**:\n\n- **N: d8-e6**: captures a pawn, but e6 is less active; doesn’t immediately threaten king\n- **N: d8-f7**: doesn’t capture, but threatens **check next move**, more forcing\n\nThus **N: d8-f7** is superior.\n\nAlso, **N: d8-b7** or **N: d8-c6** go backward, less aggressive.\n\nSo best move: **N: d8-f7**\n\nIt unleashes a threat of Nf7+ next move, disrupting black’s position.\n\nBlack will likely have to move king