ction: I have a knight on f7 — deep in enemy territory. It\'s currently on f7 and can jump to d8 (capturing black\'s rook?), e5, d6, h6, or h8.\n\nBlack’s rook on c8 (RBF1) is undefended. If I play Nf7-d8, I capture the rook — and it\'s a free piece.\n\nAlso, Nd8 attacks the black king if the rook is gone? After Nd8, black king is on e8, so knight on d8 would be adjacent — but not check since knight doesn’t attack e8 from d8? d8 to e8 is one square right — knight moves in L-shape, so no, d8 does not attack e8. But capturing the rook is still highly valuable.\n\nNow, is d8 defended? What protects black’s rook on c8? Let’s see: c8 is on the c-file. Black has pawn on c7 (PBF0)? Wait, no — in row 7: a7 has PBF0, but c7 is empty. In row 6: e6 has PBF1, but c6 is empty. So black’s rook on c8 is completely undefended. Yes!\n\nTherefore, Nf7-d8 captures a full rook for free.\n\nEvaluation:\n- N: f7-d8 — wins a rook outright, material gain (+5 points). Highly favorable.\n- Other moves like P: a2-a4 or developing pawns are slow.\n- B: g5-f6 or B: g5-e7 may pressure, but don’t win material.\n- Castling (K: g1-h1) is kingside castling? Wait — "K: g1-h1" — that’s only one square. But castling requires two squares. This suggests confusion.\n\nWait — "K: g1-h1" — king moving from g1 to h1? That’s only one square — not castling. Castling would be g1-f1 or g1-h1 with rook involvement, but standard notation would show O-O. Here, “K: g1-h1” likely means a normal king move to h1 — but that’s backward and useless.\n\nSo probably, castling is not available as a valid move option — perhaps because of prior movement or threat. But both K and Rf1 have Moved=1? No: KWF1 — king has moved? Wait: KWF1 — King White, flag Moved = 1 → yes, **king has moved**, so castling is invalid.\n\nSimilarly, rook f1 is RWF1 → has moved, so even if king hadn\'t, castling is broken.\n\nSo castling is off the table.\n\nOther moves:\n- B: g5-d8 — bishop going from g5 to d8? That’s g5-f6-e7-d8 — same square as kni