\n1 |RWF0|    |    |RWF1|    |    |KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, R: a1-c1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b3, N: b5-a3, N: b5-a7, N: b5-c3, N: b5-c7, N: b5-d4, N: b5-d6, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, R: d1-c1, R: d1-b1, R: d1-d2, R: d1-d3, R: d1-d4, R: d1-d5, R: d1-d6, R: d1-d7, R: d1-d8, R: d1-e1, R: d1-f1, K: g1-f1, K: g1-h1, P: g2-f3, P: g2-g3, B: g5-f4, B: g5-e3, B: g5-d2, B: g5-c1, B: g5-h4, B: g5-f6, B: g5-e7, B: g5-d8, B: g5-h6, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is highly asymmetric. Black has a significant material advantage in the center and on the queenside, with a rook on c8, knight on b5, bishop on f3, and pawns advancing. My position is underdeveloped, but I have all my pieces on the back rank and pawns mostly intact. The black king is exposed on c6 — an unusually advanced and vulnerable position. This suggests Black may have blundered or is in a weakened state despite material advantage. My king on g1 is safe for now, but castling is no longer possible due to the rook on d1 having moved (RWF1). However, the king hasn't moved yet (KWF1), so kingside castling might still be legal if h1 rook hasn’t moved — but it has (RWF1 at d1 implies it must have moved from h1, since original rooks are on a1 and h1 — but here a1 rook is still present? Wait — contradiction).\n\nWait: There's a **critical inconsistency** in the board state.\n\nWe have:\n- RWF0 at a1 → White rook, never moved.\n- RWF1 at d1 → Another White rook, already moved.\nBut White only has two rooks. If one is at a1 (never moved), then the other must be from h1. But d1 has RWF1 — which means it moved. So h1 rook must have moved to d1 — hence kingside castling is invalid.\n\nAlso, KWF1 at g1 → king has not moved. So theoretically, if the h1 rook hadn’t moved, castling could be possible — but it did move to d1. So castling is impossible.\n\nMoreover, Black’s king is on c6 — rank 6, file c — extremely exposed. It's deep in enemy territory with limited cover. This is highly unusual and likely exploitable.\n\nWhite has a knight on b5, which is well-placed and can attack key squares near the black king. Also, bishop on g5 puts pressure along the long diagonal.\n\nEvaluation of promising moves:\n\n1. **N: b5-a7** — Knight captures pawn on a7? But a7 has PBF0 (Black pawn, unmoved). Capturing it would win a pawn, but does it help? a7 is not near the black king. Low priority.\n\n2. **N: b5-d6** — This is **check**! The knight jumps to d6, attacking the black king on c6. Black king cannot capture the knight (no piece on c6 that can take on d6), and d6 is protected by no black piece currently. Is d6 defended?\n\nLet’s check:  \n- Black has pieces: Rook on c8, rook on g8, knight on b5 (ours?), wait — no: NWF1 on b5 is **our knight** (White). Black’s pieces:  \n  - Rooks: c8, g8  \n  - Bishop: f3  \n  - Knight: ??? Not visible — b5 is ours.  \nWait — b5 has NWF1 → White knight. So where is Black’s knight? Not visible. Possibly captured.\n\nSo Black’s king on c6 is supported only by distant pieces. Can it move? From c6, king can go to b6, b7, c7, d7, d6, d5, c5, b5 — but many of those may be attacked.\n\nN: b5-d6 delivers **check**, and likely **checkmate** or winning material because:\n- King cannot go to d6 (occupied after move).\n- No interposition against knight.\n- Only escape is moving away.\n\nCan Black king move?\n- b6: possibly safe?\n- b5: occupied by our knight? No — we’re moving from b5 to d6 — so b5 will be empty.\n- But after N: b5-d6, knight leaves b5, lands on d6.\n\nSo king on c6, attacked by knight on d6.\n\nAvailable escapes:\n- b6: is it attacked? Our bishop on g5 doesn't hit it. No other attackers obvious. Probably safe.\n- b5: not attacked? b5 is dark square. Bishop on g5 is light-squared? g5 to b5: difference of 4 files, 0 ranks — not diagonal. So no. b5 seems safe.\n- c5: possible.\n- d5: d5 is e4-b7 diagonal? g5 to d2 is possible (g5-f4-e3-d2), so g5 attacks d2, but d5? g5 to d5: delta file -3, rank 0 → no. So d5 not attacked by bishop. Knight just left b5 — no immediate threat.\n\nBut **N: b5-d6+** forces Black to move the king, losing time and potentially leading to capture.\n\nEven better: **N: b5-c7+** — knight to c7, checking the king on c6. That’s even closer.\n\nc7 is adjacent to c6 — yes, knight move from b5 to c7 is L-shaped: b to c (+1), 5 to 7 (+2) → valid.\n\nNow, **N: b5-c7+** attacks c7, which is one square above c6 — so yes, checks the king.\n\nAfter N: b5-c7+, king must move:\n- b6, b5, c5, d5, d6, d7, c7 (occupied), b7\n\nBut c7 is now occupied. Can king capture? No — knight is on c7.\n\nIs c7 defended? Yes — by the knight itself.\n\nBut more importantly: **N: b5-c7** also attacks the rook on c8!\n\nIf Black moves king, we capture rook on c8 with knight.\n\nThat’s a **fork**: after N: b5-c7, the knight attacks both king and rook.\n\nBecause after the knight moves to c7, it checks the king on c6, forcing it to move, and then next move we play N: c7xc8, capturing the rook.\n\nThis is a **winning tactic**.\n\nCompare:\n- N: b5-d6+ → check, king moves, no immediate material gain.\n- N: b5-c7+ → check **and** forks rook on c8. After king moves, we take rook.\n\nThus, **N: b5-c7** is superior.\n\nIs c7 defended by Black? Currently, what defends c7?\n- c8 rook? No — rooks don’t defend diagonally.\n- b6? Empty.\n- d6? Empty.\n- c6 king? Adjacent, but can't defend c7 without moving.\n\nNo piece on b6, d6, etc. So c7 is undefended.\n\nTherefore, **N: b5-c7+** is a legal, devastating fork.\n\nOther moves like P: a2-a4 or developing pawns are slow and miss this tactical shot.\n\nB: g5xf6 might be considered, but f6 has BBF1 — Black bishop. Capturing it gains a minor piece, but not as good as forking king and rook.\n\nReason: The knight fork on c7 wins material (at least a rook) by attacking both the black king and the undefended rook on c8, while exploiting the exposed position of the black king. It is tactically crushing.\n\nAnswer: N: b5-c7"}
