\n1 |RWF0|    |    |RWF1|    |    |KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, R: a1-c1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, N: c7-a6, N: c7-a8, N: c7-b5, N: c7-d5, N: c7-e6, N: c7-e8, R: d1-c1, R: d1-b1, R: d1-d2, R: d1-d3, R: d1-d4, R: d1-d5, R: d1-d6, R: d1-d7, R: d1-d8, R: d1-e1, R: d1-f1, K: g1-f1, K: g1-h1, P: g2-f3, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in a highly asymmetric state. My king is on g1 (KWF1), with castling rights still intact (has not moved). There are several immediate tactical and developmental considerations. The black knight on c7 (NWF1) appears to be a typo — likely meant to be NBF1 (Black Knight), given that Black is on ranks 7-8. Assuming that, it\'s a Black Knight on c7 threatening potential jumps into d5 or e6. Black also has a rook on c8 and another on g5, both inactive but potentially dangerous. Black’s king is on c6 — extremely exposed, already out of castling range and dangerously centralized without sufficient cover.\n\nMy position has undeveloped pieces: rooks on a1 and d1, king on g1. Pawns are mostly on second rank, except for g2 and h2. Notably, the pawn on c2 is adjacent to the advanced black knight on c7, which may become a target. However, Black\'s king on c6 is shockingly vulnerable — only guarded by a bishop on f3 (BBF1) and surrounded by weak squares.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **P: a2-a4 / a2-a3**: Pushing a-pawn gains space but doesn’t contribute to development or attack.\n- **P: c2-c3 / c2-c4**: c3 supports d4 advance; c4 immediately challenges d5 and opens diagonals. But c2-c4 might weaken d4.\n- **N: c7-a6 / a8 / b5 / d5 / e6 / e8**: These are Black’s knight moves — irrelevant since I\'m White.\n- **R: d1-d5 / d6 / d7 / d8**: Rook lift along d-file could pressure Black’s king on c6. d1-d5 attacks directly down the file, but no support yet.\n- **R: a1-b1 / c1**: Prepares activity, possibly supporting a queenside push or defending.\n- **K: g1-f1 / h1**: King safety move, but unnecessary unless under threat.\n- **P: g2-g3 / g2-f3**: g2-f3 develops a knight indirectly (if we had one), but currently just supports center.\n- **P: h2-h3 / h4**: h4 prepares kingside expansion, but slow.\n\nBut wait — there\'s a critical observation: **Black’s king is on c6**, and if we can open lines quickly, we may deliver a swift attack.\n\nHowever, none of my pieces are developed to exploit this yet. But **Nc3** would make sense — but I don\'t have a knight on b1 or g1. All knights are undeveloped.\n\nWait — looking again: I have a **White knight on c7?** That must be an error. In the board:\n\n> 7 |PBF0|    |NWF1| ...\n\nThat says "NWF1" — White Knight on c7? But c7 is deep in Black\'s territory, and surrounded by Black pawns. That cannot be correct unless it was a capture.\n\nBut let\'s reevaluate: Is it possible that "NWF1" means a **White Knight** on c7?\n\nThat would mean I have a knight on c7 — which implies it captured something to get there — but then why is it marked F1 (not en passant eligible)? Knights don’t use en passant.\n\nBut more importantly: if I have a knight on c7, then **N: c7-a6, c7-a8, c7-b5, c7-d5, c7-e6, c7-e8** are *my* moves — not Black\'s!\n\nAh! This is key. The piece list shows **NWF1** at c7 — meaning **White Knight on c7**, which is already advanced deep into enemy territory.\n\nSo I have a knight on c7 — excellent outpost. From c7, it can jump to:\n\n- a8 (capturing rook?),\n- b5 (attacking c3?),\n- d5 (central),\n- e8 (forking king/rook?),\n- e6 (very strong, near king),\n- a6.\n\nNow evaluating these:\n\n- **N: c7-a8**: Captures Black’s rook on a8? But a8 has nothing — board shows RBF1 on c8. So a8 is empty. So this retreats the knight uselessly.\n- **N: c7-e8**: e8 is occupied? No piece shown — so empty. But e8 is behind Black’s lines. Could fork king if king were on d8, but king is on c6.\n- **N: c7-e6**: e6 is empty. From e6, the knight attacks d4, f4, c5, d8, f8 — but more importantly, **attacks c5, d4, f4, and crucially, d8 and f8** — not directly threatening king on c6.\n- **N: c7-d5**: d5 is empty. From d5, knight attacks c7 (self), e7, f6, b6, b4, c3 — and **f6, e7**, etc. But not directly hitting king.\n- **N: c7-b5**: b5 is empty. Attacks a7, c7, d4, d6, a3 — again, not impactful.\n- **N: c7-a6**: a6 is empty. Retreats toward edge.\n\nBut none seem immediately crushing.\n\nWait — what about **Nc7-e6+**? Is that check?\n\nKing on c6. e6 is two files right and zero ranks up/down? c to e is +2, 6 to 6 = same. So knight from e6 to c6 is L-shaped? e6 to c6 is -2 files, same rank — not a knight move.\n\nKnight from e6 to c6: Δfile= -2, Δrank=0 → invalid.\n\nFrom e6, knight reaches:\n- f8 (+1,+2)\n- g7 (+2,+1)\n- g5 (+2,-1)\n- f4 (+1,-2)\n- d4 (-1,-2)\n- c5 (-2,-1)\n- c7 (-2,+1)\n- d8 (-1,+2)\n\nc6 is not among them.\n\nWhat about **Nc7-b5**? From b5, knight reaches:\n- a7 (-1,+2)\n- c7 (+1,+2)\n- d6 (+2,+1)\n- d4 (+2,-1)\n- c3 (+1,-2)\n- a3 (-1,-2)\n- b7 (0,+2) — no\nWait: from b5, knight to c6? Δfile=+1, Δrank=+1 — not L-shaped.\n\nNo knight move from c7 hits c6.\n\nBut wait — is the king really on c6? And unprotected?\n\nLet’s see: **KBF1** on c6 — Black King.\n\nSurrounding squares:\n- b6: empty\n- b5: ?\n- b7: PBF0 — black pawn\n- c7: NWF1 — my knight!\n- d7: empty\n- d6: empty\n- d5: empty\n- c5: PBF1 — black pawn\n\nSo knight on c7 is **adjacent** to Black king on c6 — but knight doesn\'t attack adjacent squares.\n\nThe only way to attack king is via forks or checks.\n\nBut perhaps **Nc7-e8**? e8