nSo K: g1-f1 or h1 are just king walks — risky.\n\nThus, avoid unnecessary king moves.\n\nNow, rook on d7 is under attack — **must move**.\n\nBest options:\n- R: d7-d5 — controls center, threatens pawn on f5? No, f5 is empty. But prepares support.\n- R: d7-d4 — active, eyes f4 where Black bishop is?\nWait — Black bishop is on f3? Board: f3 has BBF1 — yes, Black bishop on f3.\n\nR: d7-d4 attacks f4, but not directly threatening f3.\n\nBut more importantly, retreating to d2 or d1 keeps it on the back rank.\n\nBut better: **R: d7-g7** — moves to g7, which attacks the Black bishop on f3 via g7-g3? Not really.\n\nWait: diagonal? No, rook moves straight.\n\nR: d7-g7 → horizontal move to g-file.\n\ng7 is empty. Then rook would be on g7, facing toward h7 and possibly pressuring g-file.\n\nBut Black has no pieces on g-file immediately.\n\nAlternatively: **R: d7-d3** — attacks the bishop on f3? No, d3 is not aligned.\n\nd7-d3 is down the d-file — ends at d3.\n\nBut f3 is diagonal.\n\nNo direct threat.\n\nActually, safest and most flexible: **R: d7-d2** — retreats to pawn shield, protected by pawns, keeps connection with rook on a1.\n\nOr **R: d7-d1**, connecting rooks.\n\nBut rook on a1 is also White — so **R: d7-d1** connects both rooks — very strong.\n\nThat’s excellent: doubles rooks on first rank, increases coordination, and removes rook from attack.\n\nAlso, after R: d7-d1, the rook is safe and supports any central play.\n\nOther options:\n- P: a2-a3 or a2-a4 — preparing b4 or development, but not urgent.\n- P: g2-f3 — kicks the Black bishop on f3? Yes! And opens diagonal for bishop or queen.\n\nWait — **P: g2-f3** — captures **en passant**? No, Black didn’t advance two squares.\n\nBut **f3 is occupied by BBF1 (Black Bishop)** — so **P: g2-f3 is a capture!**\n\nYes! The move **P: g2-f3** means pawn from g2 takes on f3 — capturing the Black bishop.\n\nIs that allowed?\n\nCurrent board:\n- g2: PWF0 (White pawn)\n- f3: BBF1 (Black bishop)\n\nSo yes — pawn on g2 can capture 