\n1 |RWF0|    |    |RWF1|    |    |KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, R: a1-c1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c3, R: d1-c1, R: d1-b1, R: d1-d2, R: d1-d3, R: d1-d4, R: d1-e1, R: d1-f1, N: d5-b4, N: d5-b6, N: d5-c3, N: d5-c7, N: d5-e3, N: d5-e7, N: d5-f4, N: d5-f6, P: f3-g4, P: f3-f4, K: g1-f1, K: g1-g2, K: g1-h1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is highly asymmetric. My king is on g1 (KWF1), and I have two rooks on a1 and d1, both unmoved or partially moved. The knight on d5 is mine (NWF1), already active in the center. Black has a dangerous setup: their king is on c6 (KBF1), extremely exposed and close to the center, with a rook on c8 (RBF1) and another on g5 (RBF1). Black’s pawn structure is weak, especially on the queenside and center. My pieces are somewhat undeveloped, but I have a strong central knight on d5 that can exert immediate pressure.\n\nNotably, Black's king is on c6 — an unusual and vulnerable position. It has little cover and no nearby defenders. My knight on d5 is just one square away from delivering a fork or even a check. Moves like Nc7+ or Nb4 could target key squares near the black king.\n\nEvaluation:\n- N: d5-b6 — threatens nothing directly; b6 is empty and not particularly useful.\n- N: d5-c7+ — this checks the black king! The knight moves to c7, attacking the king on c6. Black must respond immediately. After Nc7+, Black cannot capture the knight (no piece can), and the king has limited escape: Kb8, Kb6, or Kd6. However, after Kb8, my rook on a1 can potentially double on the c-file or follow up with Rc1-c6, winning material. This is a very strong tactical shot.\n- N: d5-b4 — targets c2 indirectly but doesn't create immediate threats.\n- P: a2-a4 / a3 — passive, not urgent.\n- R: a1-b1 / c1 — slow development.\n- K: g1-f1/h1/g2 — unnecessary king movement.\n\nThe most forcing and promising move is **N: d5-c7**, which gives check and exploits the poor placement of the black king. It wins at least material (likely leading to a decisive advantage) because after Kb8, I can play Ra1-c1, threatening Rc6 or even Rxc7 if recaptured.\n\nReason: The move **N: d5-c7** delivers check, forces Black into a defensive-only mode, and likely wins material due to the exposed black king. It maximizes initiative and capitalizes on a rare positional blunder by Black.\n\nAnswer: N: d5-c7"}
