aptures PBF1 on b3. That’s a free pawn! Why isn’t this obvious?\n- After P: c2-b3, black loses a pawn, and I gain material. Moreover, it opens the c-file or supports central control.\n- But wait: is b3 occupied? Yes — row 3: b3 has PBF1. And c2 has PWF0 — a white pawn that hasn’t moved. So yes, it can capture diagonally forward to b3.\n- Pawns capture diagonally — so from c2, capturing to b3 is **backward** — c2 to b3 is left and forward? Files: c to b is left, rank 2 to 3 is up — so yes, forward-left. That's a legal capture direction for white pawn.\n- So P: c2-b3 is a valid **capture** of a black pawn.\n- This wins material immediately — always good.\n- Compare to R: a1-a3+ — which gives check but risks losing the rook to b3xa3 if king moves.\n- But after R: a1-a3+, if black plays Kb5, then rook is still attacked by c4 pawn? c4 pawn can't reach a3. Only b3 pawn can capture on a3.\n- a3 is on file a, b3 is on b — so b3 pawn can capture on a3? Yes — diagonally forward: b3 to a4 would be capture, but b3 to a2 is backward. Wait: no.\n- Pawn on b3 (black) — color black — moves **downward**, so its capture directions are to a2 and c2 (diagonally down).\n- Therefore, black pawn on b3 can **only** capture on a2 or c2 — not on a3 or c4.\n- So a3 is **not** under attack by any black pawn.\n- c4 pawn (black) is on c4 — moves downward — captures on b3 and d3.\n- So b3 and d3 are its targets — not a3.\n- Therefore, **no black piece can capture on a3**.\n- So if I play R: a1-a3, the rook cannot be captured by any pawn.\n- King on a5 — adjacent squares: a4, a6, b4, b5, b6? But only within bounds.\n- From a5, king can go to a4, a6, b4, b5, b6? b6 is up — but black moves down? No — king movement is independent of color.\n- King moves one square in any direction — so from a5: a4, a6, b4, b5, b6? b6 is (b,6) — one up, one right — yes, knight? No, king: one square any direction.\n- So a5 → a4 (down), a6 (up), b5 (up-right), b4 (down-right), b6? b6 is two up, one right — no. Only one 