\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c4-c5, P: d4-d5, K: f2-e1, K: f2-e2, K: f2-e3, K: f2-f1, K: f2-f3, K: f2-g1, K: f2-g2, K: f2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is nearly empty—only a few pawns and the two kings remain. My king is on f2 (KWF1), and Black’s king is on e5 (KBF1). There are three white pawns: on b2 (PWF0), c4 (PWF1), and d4 (PWF1), and one on h2 (PWF0). No immediate threats exist, and Black has no pawns or pieces left besides the king. The goal now is to promote one of my pawns to a queen (or other piece) to deliver checkmate, as checkmating with just a king and pawns vs king is achievable with proper technique.\n\nEvaluation: \n- Advancing pawns is key. The b2 and h2 pawns are on their starting squares and can move two steps (b2-b4/h2-h4) or one (b2-b3/h2-h3). The c4 and d4 pawns are already advanced but only one square forward.\n- Central pawns (c4, d4) are closer to promotion (only 4 ranks from queening), while b2 and h2 are five ranks away. Pushing c4 or d4 would be more efficient.\n- However, **d4-d5** and **c4-c5** are both valid moves, advancing central pawns.\n- But wait: the move list only allows **c4-c5** and **d4-d5**, meaning both are eligible.\n- Comparing c4 and d4: c4 is closer to my king (on f2), which could help support its advance. d4 is more central but slightly less supported.\n- However, pushing **d4-d5** opens the diagonal for potential king activity and controls more space.\n- Alternatively, moving the king (**K: f2-e2**, **f2-g2**, etc.) may help centralize it to support pawn advancement. The king must assist in escorting a pawn to promotion in endgames.\n- Best practice in king and pawn endgames is to centralize the king and support a passed pawn.\n\nBut note: currently, none of my pawns are truly "passed" yet, but since Black has no pawns, all are effectively passed once advanced.\n\nThe most effective plan is to push a central pawn supported by the king. Among the options, **d4-d5** advances a central pawn, gaining space and bringing it closer to promotion. Alternatively, **c4-c5** does similar, but d-pawn is more central.\n\nAlso, **king moves** like **f2-e2** or **f2-g2** help activate the king. But pawn moves gain tempo.\n\nHowever, **pushing d4-d5** immediately increases pressure and brings the pawn closer to queening in just 4 moves (d5-d6-d7-d8=Q). Similarly, c4-c5 would take same number.\n\nBut d4-d5 places a pawn on d5, which is directly in front of the black king\'s position (e5), potentially restricting Black king movement if he tries to block.\n\nBlack king on e5 can easily capture any isolated pawn unless supported.\n\nThus, the king must advance. So perhaps preparing with a king move is better?\n\nWait: after d4-d5, Black king can play Kxd5, eliminating the pawn. Same for c4-c5.\n\nSo we must avoid premature pushes without king support.\n\nTherefore, best is to first **activate the king**.\n\nCurrent king on f2. Ideal path is toward center: e3, d4, c5, etc.\n\nFrom f2, possible king moves: e1, e2, e3, f1, f3, g1, g2, g3.\n\nBest among these: **K: f2-e3** or **f2-g2**? \n\n- e3 is central, supports d4 and c4 pawns.\n- g2 is safer, prepares h-pawn push.\n\nBut the **central pawns (c4, d4)** are more valuable in endgames due to easier king support and queening with less risk.\n\nSo king should head to **e3 → d4 → c5**, supporting c/d-pawns.\n\nThus, **K: f2-e3** is excellent: centralizing, supporting both c4 and d4.\n\nAlternatively, **K: f2-e2** also good, but e3 is better.\n\nLooking at valid moves: **K: f2-e3** is available.\n\nIs there a pawn move that cannot be taken back? Pushing **d4-d5** risks losing the pawn to ...Kxd5, leaving me with fewer pawns.\n\nI have four pawns. Losing one is not fatal, but unnecessary.\n\nBetter to keep pawns intact and use king to create a winning position.\n\nHence, **king activation is priority**.\n\nAmong king moves, **f2-e3** is strongest: approaches center, supports c4/d4, threatens to support a pawn storm.\n\nReason: Centralizing the king is crucial in king and pawn endgames. With the king on e3, I can support the advance of either c4 or d4 pawns safely, prevent Black king from capturing them, and aim for promotion. Premature pawn pushes risk simplification into a drawish position.\n\nAnswer: K: f2-e3'}
