\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c5-c6, P: d4-d5, K: e3-d2, K: e3-d3, K: e3-e2, K: e3-e4, K: e3-f2, K: e3-f3, K: e3-f4, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is nearly empty. My king is on e3, and Black’s king is on e7. There are only a few pawns left: White has pawns on b2, c5, d4, and h2; Black has no visible pawns or pieces besides the king. All my pawns are eligible to move, and none have moved yet except possibly those already marked as moved (c5 and d4 are marked PWF1, so they’ve moved before). The kings have both moved (KWF1 and KBF1), so castling is not possible for either side. There is no immediate threat of check or checkmate.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Advancing pawns can help control space and potentially promote. With limited material, pawn promotion will likely decide the game.\n- Moving the king toward the center (e.g., e3–e4, e3–d3) could centralize it, which is valuable in endgames.\n- Pushing a pawn two squares may offer rapid advancement but risks overextension without support.\n- The pawns on c5 and d4 are already advanced and one square away from promotion (c5 to c6, d4 to d5), but they are blocked by nothing—so advancing them gains ground.\n- The b2 and h2 pawns are on their starting squares and can move two steps (b2-b4/h2-h4) or one (b2-b3/h2-h3).\n\nKey considerations:\n- Promoting a pawn requires careful king coordination. Centralizing the king (e.g., Ke3–e4) supports multiple pawn advances.\n- However, with no enemy pieces visible other than the king, there's little risk of tactical tricks.\n- The most urgent productive moves are pushing the already advanced pawns (c5→c6, d4→d5), as they are closer to queening.\n- Among these, d4–d5 advances the pawn to the 5th rank, controlling more space and getting one step from the 6th. Similarly, c5–c6 does the same on the c-file.\n- But d4–d5 opens the possibility of supporting a future c5–c6 push with better king activity.\n- Alternatively, b2–b4 or h2–h4 could be useful flank pawns, but they are much farther from promotion.\n\nBest progress is made by advancing central pawns and centralizing the king.\n\nComparing promising options:\n- P: d4-d5 — Strong. Gains space, keeps options open, prepares for potential breakthrough.\n- P: c5-c6 — Also strong. One step from promotion (c6-c7 next).\n- K: e3-e4 — Good king activity, supports central pawns.\n- P: b2-b3 or P: h2-h3 — Slower, less impactful.\n\nBetween c5–c6 and d4–d5, c5–c6 brings the pawn to the 6th rank, just two steps from queening. That pawn would then be very dangerous if supported.\n\nHowever, after c5–c6, Black’s king may drift toward it. We need to coordinate our own king.\n\nBut note: c5 is already marked PWF1 — it has moved, so it must have come from c7 or c6 originally. c5–c6 is only one square forward.\n\nWait: pawns promote at the 8th rank. From c5, it needs c5→c6→c7→c8 = three moves to promote. Same for d4→d5→d6→d7→d8? No: d4 to d5 is one move, then d5 to d6 (2), d6 to d7 (3), d7 to d8 (4). So c5 is closer (3 moves) than d4 (4 moves).\n\nSo c5–c6 reduces that to two moves from promotion.\n\nThus, c5–c6 is better than d4–d5 in terms of speed.\n\nMoreover, after c5–c6, the pawn is on c6, threatening c7, and can be supported by the king from d5 or b5.\n\nTherefore, c5–c6 is a high-priority move.\n\nAlso, the b2 and h2 pawns are on starting squares. Moving them two squares (b2–b4, h2–h4) gains more space than one-square moves.\n\nBut again, they are far from promotion.\n\nKing moves like e3–e4 help, but pawn moves make more progress in this race.\n\nReason: The key to winning such endgames is promoting first. The c5 pawn is the most advanced and closest to queening. Advancing it to c6 maximizes pressure and minimizes time to promotion. While king centralization is useful, immediate pawn advancement takes priority when the opponent has no counterplay (Black has no pawns or active pieces). Thus, pushing c5–c6 is optimal.\n\nAnswer: P: c5-c6"}
