ectly attacks f5 and controls central squares.\n  - Q: e7-g5 puts the queen one square away from f5 and threatens mate ideas if supported.\n  - Q: e7-d6 also pressures f5 via diagonal and supports king advance.\n- **King moves**: Bringing the king closer is essential for checkmating. e3-d4 or e3-f4 would be ideal, but not all are available.\n  - K: e3-d4 is legal and advances toward c5/d5/e5/f4 — all useful squares.\n  - K: e3-f3 or K: e3-d3 centralizes slightly.\n  - K: e3-e2 or K: e3-d2 retreats, which is suboptimal.\n- **Pawn moves**:\n  - P: b2-b3 or P: b2-b4 — delays promotion, minimal immediate benefit.\n  - P: h2-h3 or P: h2-h4 — same; h4 might support future g4 push, but very slow.\n\nBest plan: Use queen and king coordination to drive the black king toward the edge and deliver checkmate. Immediate queen checks or strong central control moves are best.\n\nTop candidate moves:\n1. **Q: e7-e5** — Directly attacks the black king on f5 (if it were on e5, it would be check, but f5 is adjacent). Wait — e7-e5 does *not* attack f5. e7-e5 is on the same file, but f5 is one file over. So no attack. But e5 controls d5, e4, e6, f5? No — queen on e5 attacks rank 5 and file e. So yes — **queen on e5 attacks f5** (same rank), since f5 is on rank 5. Yes! So Q: e7-e5 puts the black king in **check**!\n\nWait: Queen on e7 to e5 — same file, so vertical move. From e7 to e5 is down two squares. Then on e5, it controls entire rank 5 — including f5. So yes, **Q: e7-e5 gives check to the black king on f5**.\n\nThis is critical.\n\nAfter Q: e7-e5+, black king must move. Possible escapes: f5-e4, f5-f4, f5-g4, f5-g5, f5-g6, f5-f6, f5-e6, etc., depending on threats.\n\nBut more importantly — this is a **check**, and likely leads to faster king hunt.\n\nCompare to:\n- Q: e7-g5+ — also checks: g5 is on same rank as f5? No — g5 is rank 5, f5 is rank 5 — yes! So g5 is on rank 5, so Q: e7-g5 goes to g5, which is on rank 5 — so yes, attacks f5. Also a check.\n\nSimilarly:\n- Q: e7-c5 — c5 is rank 5 — attacks f5? c5 to f5 is three files over — yes, same rank — so yes, horizontal attack. So Q: e7-c5 also gives check.\n\nSo multiple queen moves give check:\n- Q: e7-e5 (check)\n- Q: e7-g5 (check)\n- Q: e7-c5 (check)\n- Q: e7-d5? Not listed — not a valid move.\nValid checking moves: e7-e5, e7-g5, e7-c5, e7-f6? f6 — is that same diagonal? e7 to f6 is diagonal, and f6 to f5 is vertical — no, f6 does not attack f5 unless same file or rank or diagonal. f6 and f5 are same file — yes! So queen on f6 attacks f5 vertically. So Q: e7-f6 also attacks f5 — and it's in the move list.\n\nIndeed, Q: e7-f6 is listed — and f6 is on file f, so yes — attacks f5 directly. So also a check.\n\nSo all of these queen moves give check:\n- Q: e7-c5 (rank 5)\n- Q: e7-e5 (rank 5)\n- Q: e7-g5 (rank 5)\n- Q: e7-f6 (file f)\n\nAll four are checks.\n\nNow, which is best?\n\nLet’s evaluate:\n\n- **Q: e7-f6+** — attacks along the file. King on f5 cannot go to f4 or f6 (but can go to e4, e5, g4, g5, etc.). After Qf6+, Kb moves to e4, g4, g5, e5? But then Qf6 controls f-file and 6th rank. Strong.\n\nBut better: **Q: e7-e5+** — places queen in center of board, controls entire rank 5 and file e. After Ke5+, Black king must leave f5. Options: e4, e6, f4, g4, g5, g6, f6, e6 — but many are attacked.\n\nBut even better: **Q: e7-g5+** — queen goes to g5, attacking f5 from the side. Then after Kg4??, Qg5# — wait, no: if king goes to g4, queen on g5 gives check — but king on g4, queen on g5 — same file? No, queen on g5, king on g4 — same file — yes, so queen attacks g4. So if king moves to g4, Qg5 is check, but not mate — king could have come from f5 to g4, then Qg5+ forces it to g3/h4/h5/g5/etc.? But not helpful.\n\nActually, **Q: e7-e5+** is powerful because it cuts off the king’s escape downward (rank 5 blocked), and my king can advance.\n\nBut consider **king move first**: Should I prepare by bringing my king closer?\n\nK: e3-d4 — centralizing, threatening to march to d5 or e5. But not urgent — 