agonal? e5 to h4: +3 files, -1 rank — no — not diagonal. So h4 not attacked — safe.\n\nSo king escapes to g4, h4, g6, h6.\n\nThen game continues, but no immediate win.\n\nBut what about **Q: e7-g5+**?\n\nQe7-g5 — same rank 5 — so attacks f5 — check.\n\nNow, where can king go?\n\n- e4: not same rank — g5 to e4: different rank/file — diagonal? g5 to e4: -2 files, -1 rank — no — not diagonal. So e4 safe?\nWait: queen on g5 attacks:\n- Rank 5: all files\n- File g: all ranks\n- Diagonals: f6, e7, d8 and f4, e3, d2, c1 and h6 and h4\n\nSo f5 is on rank 5 — attacked.\n\nAfter Qg5+, king on f5 must move.\n\nCan it go to e4? e4: same rank? No (5 vs 4). Same file? No. Diagonal? g5 to e4: -2 files, -1 rank — not diagonal. So e4 not attacked — safe.\n\ng4? same file — g4 is file g — yes, attacked.\n\nh4? diagonal — g5 to h4: +1 file, -1 rank — yes — attacked.\n\nf4? same rank? f4 is rank 4 — no. File? f — no. Diagonal? g5 to f4: -1 file, -1 rank — yes — attacked.\n\ne5? rank 5 — attacked.\n\nf6? rank 5 — attacked.\n\ng6? file g — attacked.\n\nSo only safe squares: **e4**, **h5**, **h6**\n\nh5: rank 5 — attacked.\n\nh6: not rank 5, not file g, diagonal? g5 to h6: +1 file, +1 rank — yes — attacked.\n\nSo only **e4** is safe.\n\nSo after Qg5+, Black king can **only go to e4**.\n\nThat’s a massive restriction!\n\nThen after:\n1. Qe7-g5+ Kf5-e4\n2. Now I play Kd4! — attacking e4.\n\nNow Black king has no escape:\n- d4? attacked by my king\n- d5? not yet\n- e3? Qg5 attacks e3? g5 to e3: -2 files, -2 ranks — diagonal — yes — attacked\n- f3? g5 to f3: -1 file, -2 ranks — no\n- f4? g5 to f4: -1 file, -1 rank — diagonal — yes — attacked\n- e5? rank 5 — attacked\n- f5? rank 5 — attacked\n\nSo only possible: f3? But f3 not attacked by queen? g5 to f3: file difference -1, rank difference -2 — not straight — so not attacked.\n\nAnd not occupied.\n\nSo Ke4-f3 is possible.\n\nThen Qg5+? No — f3 not attacked.\n\nBut I can play Qg5-e5+! — attacking f3? e5 to f3: +1 file, -2 ranks — no — not attacked.\n\nBetter: after Kd4, if Ke4-f3, then Qg5-g3+! — attacks f3? g3 to f3: same rank — yes! So Qg5-g3+ Kf3, then Qg3-g2+ or something.\n\nBut simpler: after Qg5+ Ke4, play **Qg5-e5+!** — check on the file.\n\nQe5+ Ke4, but e4 is on e-file — yes, so Qe5 attacks e4 — check.\n\nSo 2. Qe5+! Ke4-e3? Then Qe5-e1#? No — e1 not controlled.\n\nKe4-e3: then Qe5-e1+? But e1 is far.\n\nBut Ke4 cannot go to e3 — Qe5 attacks e3? e5 to e3 — same file — yes — so e3 attacked.\n\nd4? occupied by my king.\n\nd5? not attacked — possible.\n\nSo Ke4-d5.\n\nThen Kd4-c5, etc.\n\nBut still winning.\n\nBut back to **Q: e7-g5+** — it forces the king to e4, which is a weak square, and I can follow with Kd4 and Qe5 or Qf4, leading to quick mate.\n\nCompared to Qe5+, which allows king to escape to g4 or g6 — worse.\n\nTherefore, **Q: e7-g5+** is superior.\n\nAlso, **Q: e7-c5+** — similar idea.\n\nQc5+ — attacks f5 (same rank).\n\nWhere can king go?\n\n- e4: not attacked? c5 to e4: +2 files, -1 rank — no — not straight — safe?\nc5 attacks:\n- Rank 5\n- File c\n- Diagonals: b6, a7, d6, e7, f8 and b4, a3, d4, e3, f2, g1\n\nSo e4: not rank 5, not file c, diagonal? c5 to e4: +2 files, -1 rank — no — so e4 safe.\n\ng4: not attacked — safe.\n\ng6: safe.\n\nBut f4? c5 to f4: +3 files, -1 rank — no — not attacked — safe.\n\nSo many escapes.\n\nWorse than Qg5+.\n\nSimilarly, Qf6+ — e7 to f6 — check on file f.\n\nAfter Qf6+, Kf5-e4? — e4 not on file f or rank 6 — diagonal? f6 to e4: -1 file, -2 ranks — no — safe.\n\ng4? f6 to g4: +1 file, -2 ranks — no — safe.\n\ng5? rank 6? no — file? no — diagonal? f6 to g5: +1, -1 — yes — attacked.\n\ne5? f6 to e5: -1, -1 — diagonal — attacked.\n\nf4? same file — attacked.\n\nSo escapes: e4, g4, h5, h6, etc.\n\nToo many.\n\nOnly **Q: e7-g5+** restricts king to **only e4**.\n\nTherefore, it is the best move.\n\nReason: Q: e7-g5+ forces the black king to e4, the only safe square, severely limiting its mobility and setting up a quick mating net with Kd4 and queen maneuvers.\n\nAnswer: Q: e7-g5"}
