e time.\n- Qg5-g8 threatens checkmate on g7 or h8 if the black king were near, but currently it's not effective since Black can simply move away.\n- Most promising is using the queen to restrict the black king’s movement or supporting a pawn breakthrough.\n\nHowever, the most forcing and winning idea is to use the queen to dominate the black king. Qg5-d5 cuts off the black king from the queenside and central files, preparing for a potential Kb2-c3-b4-a5 type of king march to support the b-pawn. Alternatively, Qg5-a5 puts immediate pressure along the 5th rank, threatening mate on a7 or b8 and restricting Ke6.\n\nBut even better: **Qg5-g8**! This looks surprising, but consider: after Qg5-g8, the black king is in danger of being mated on h8 or forced into zugzwang. If Black’s king moves to f6, then Qg8-g7+ forces it back, and White can bring the king up. But more importantly, **Qg5-d8** or **Qg5-e7** may be stronger for cutting escape routes.\n\nWait — actually, **Qg5-e7** attacks the square e7, which is critical because if the black king goes to d6 or f6, it’s under pressure. But better yet: **Qg5-d5+** checks the black king!\n\nLet’s check: Qg5-d5+? From g5 to d5 is diagonal? No — g5 to d5 is three files left and same rank — horizontal. Yes! Queen can move horizontally. So Qg5-d5 is along the 5th rank. Is e6 on d5’s line? d5 is file d, rank 5. Black king is on e6 — not aligned. So Qd5 does not give check.\n\nBut **Qg5-e5**? That would be on the same rank as the black king? No — black king is on e6, so e5 is directly below. So Qe5 controls e5 and attacks e6 from below. That could be useful.\n\nBut the strongest practical move is **advancing a pawn with tempo or space gain**, especially since the opponent cannot immediately counterattack.\n\nBest plan: Push b2-b4, gaining queenside space, opening lines for the king to advance via c3, b4, a5, etc., and prepare b4-b5-b6-b7-b8=Q. This is a clear path to promotion. The h-pawn is farther and less likely to promote first.\n\nReason: Among all options, **b2-b4** maximizes progress toward a decisive advantage by accelerating the b-pawn’s journey to promotion, controlling more space, and enabling the king to follow via c3 and d4. It’s safe and consistent with endgame principles: push the pawn farthest from the kings first (but here both kings are near center; however, the b-pawn is unopposed).\n\nAnswer: P: b2-b4"}
