\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: b4-b5, K: e3-d2, K: e3-d3, K: e3-d4, K: e3-e2, K: e3-e4, K: e3-f2, K: e3-f3, K: e3-f4, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, with only my king (KWF1) on e3 and a pawn on b4 (PWF1) and h2 (PWF0). The Black king is on b7 (KBF1), far from the center. My king is active but must avoid allowing stalemate or passive play. Black has no pawns or pieces visible — likely an endgame scenario where I must promote a pawn to win.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: b4-b5 – Advances the queenside pawn, but it's isolated and far from support; Black king can easily stop it with ...Kb6 or ...Kc7.\n- P: h2-h3 – Prepares to push h4, but slow; doesn’t gain space immediately.\n- P: h2-h4 – Gains space, activates the h-pawn toward promotion; creates a potential passer if supported.\n- King moves (e3 to d2, d3, d4, e2, e4, f2, f3, f4) – All involve centralization or supporting the h-pawn. The most useful are those that support h2-h4 and eventual h4-h5, pushing the pawn forward.\n\nThe key winning plan is to advance a pawn to promotion while keeping the king active. The h-pawn is on the edge and harder for the black king to reach quickly, especially since it’s on b7. Pushing h2-h4 now starts creating a passed pawn on the kingside.\n\nReason: P: h2-h4 is the most effective move to begin creating a passed pawn on the h-file. It gains space, doesn’t block future development, and prepares h5 and h6, potentially leading to promotion unless opposed. Meanwhile, my king can march toward the center or kingside to support it. In contrast, b4-b5 is less promising because the black king is closer to the queenside and can blockade easily.\n\nAnswer: P: h2-h4"}
