t's *my* turn.\n\nWait: current position: b5 has PWF1 (White Pawn), b6 is empty, b7 has KBF1 (Black King). So if I play b5-b6, the pawn moves to b6, and the Black king is on b7 — directly above it. Then Black could capture the pawn via Kb7-c6 or Kb7-b6? No: kings can't jump; but Kb7-b6 would be a capture if the pawn were on b6. Wait — after b5-b6, pawn is on b6, king on b7 — adjacent. Then Black can play Kb7-b6 and capture the pawn!\n\nSo b5-b6?? loses the pawn to Kb7xb6.\n\nTherefore, **b5-b6 is a blunder**.\n\nInstead, I must prevent the black king from getting involved. But the only way to make progress is to push the h-pawn, which is far from the black king.\n\nh4-h5 advances the h-pawn, starting its journey. It's safe — the black king is nowhere near. This pawn cannot be stopped in time.\n\nThus:\n- b5-b6: loses pawn to Kb7xb6\n- h4-h5: safely advances a distant passed pawn\n\nKing moves do nothing productive right now.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to push the h-pawn.\n\nReason: Pushing the b-pawn loses it immediately to the black king. The h-pawn is far from the black king and can advance safely to promotion. Therefore, h4-h5 starts that process without risk.\n\nAnswer: P: h4-h5"}
