\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: b5-b6, K: e3-d2, K: e3-d3, K: e3-d4, K: e3-e2, K: e3-e4, K: e3-f2, K: e3-f3, K: e3-f4, P: h7-h8-q, P: h7-h8-n, P: h7-h8-b, P: h7-h8-r]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is nearly empty. My king is on e3, and I have a pawn on h7 that is one square away from promoting. The black king is on b8, far away from the action. Black has a pawn on b5, which just moved to b5 (marked as PWF1 — meaning it has moved, but not two squares). However, there are no immediate threats to my king or my pawns. The key opportunity lies in promoting my h-pawn.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Promoting the h7 pawn (h7-h8) is an immediate tactical priority, as it will become a queen, knight, bishop, or rook. A queen promotion gives maximum flexibility and power.\n- The only other legal moves are king moves (e3 to various adjacent squares), which do not improve my position significantly.\n- Delaying promotion risks nothing here, but since there's no danger and no benefit from delaying, immediate promotion is optimal.\n- Among promotions, queen is strongest: it provides the best chance to deliver checkmate quickly even if black tries to maneuver.\n\nReason: Promoting the h-pawn to a queen immediately increases material advantage to overwhelming levels, especially with the black king passive on b8. There is no risk in advancing, and the reward is decisive.\n\nAnswer: P: h7-h8-q"}
