educing distance is key.\n\nPromising moves:\n- K: f4-e5 — advances toward center, reduces distance to f7\n- K: f4-f5 — directly approaches, same file\n- K: f4-g5 — flanks around, prepares to cut off retreat\n\nBut note: f4 to e5 places White king on e5, two squares away diagonally from f7 — Black king remains free.\nf4 to f5 brings king to same file, one rank closer.\nf4 to g5 places on g5 — further away laterally.\n\nClosest approach is f4-f5, reducing distance.\n\nHowever, **K: f4-e4** or **K: f4-g4** do not improve position.\n\nBest progress is **K: f4-f5**, advancing up the board, threatening to gain opposition.\n\nBut wait — after f4-f5, Black plays f7-f6, and we repeat. Alternatively, f4-e5 is more central.\n\nIn king vs king endgames, the **opposition** is key when the kings are aligned. Here, White wants to force the Black king backward.\n\nThe most direct and effective advance is:\n\n**K: f4-f5** — this gains ground, keeps flexibility, and pressures Black to retreat (f7-f6).\n\nAlternatively, **K: f4-e5** or **K: f4-g5** also approach, but f5 is central and strong.\n\nBut let’s calculate distance:\n- From f4 to f5: now on f5, distance to f7 is 2 ranks apart — not adjacent.\n- But after f5, Black must respond, and White continues advancing.\n\nActually, **K: f4-e5** is better? e5 is closer in king-move terms to both f7 and potential chasing paths.\n\nWait — Euclidean (king-move) distance from f4 to f7 is 3 (f4→e5→f6→f7). From f5, it\'s 2 (f5→f6→f7). So advancing to f5 shortens path.\n\nBut f4 to f5 is allowed and improves position.\n\nAll of e3, e4, e5, f3, f5, g3, g4, g5 are options.\n\nBut f3, g3, e3, g4 are sideways or backward — weakening.\n\nSo forward moves: e5, f5, g5.\n\nAmong these:\n- e5: controls d6, e6, f6, d5, etc., supports central approach\n- f5: directly threatens to occupy f6\n- g5: less direct\n\nBest is **f5**, as it directly challenges the file and forces Black to react.\n\nAlso, **K: f4-f5** is clean and central.\n\nReason: Advancing the king to f5 puts maximum pressure on the Black king, reduces its space, and aims to drive it toward the edge. It\'s the most direct route to gaining positional dominance in the endgame.\n\nAnswer: K: f4-f5'}
