ank difference = 0 → not adjacent. Wait: h7 and e7 are three files apart, so not adjacent. But let's compute:\n\nBlack king on e7 (file 5, rank 7), White king moving to h7 (file 8, rank 7): difference of 3 files, so not adjacent. So h7 is safe. But is it useful?\n\nWait: K: g6-h7 — this is a valid king move (one diagonal)? g6 to h7 is one square diagonally (right and up). Yes, valid.\n\nBut now evaluate: h7 is on the edge, and far from e7. After g6→h7, White king is on h7, Black king still centralized. Not ideal.\n\nBest plan: Centralize and cut off escape routes. The key is to use opposition when possible. Since Black king is on e7, White wants to get to d6, e6, or f6 to gain opposition and push Black back.\n\nCurrently, White king on g6 can reach f6 in one move — but f6 is not in the list. Wait: valid moves listed are:\n[K: g6-f5, K: g6-g5, K: g6-g7, K: g6-h5, K: g6-h6, K: g6-h7]\n\nWait — why isn't g6-f6 a valid move? That’s one square diagonally left and down — should be allowed.\n\nBut according to the valid moves provided, it's not listed. So either there's an error, or I must trust the given list.\n\nSo among the **given valid moves**, we must choose.\n\nLet’s reevaluate based on proximity to Black king and ability to restrict movement.\n\n- g6→f5: brings White king to f5 (file 6, rank 5). Distance to e7: one diagonal up-left. Controls e4, e5, e6, f4, f6, g4, g5, g6. From f5, it exerts pressure upward toward e6, d6, etc. Eventually supports entry into the 6th rank.\n\n- g6→g5: stays on g-file, drops to rank 5. Less progress.\n\n- g6→g7: moves up to g7. Now on same rank as Black king. Controls f7, f8, f6, g6, h6, h7, h8. From g7, it eyes f7 and h7, and can later go to f6 or f8. Also, f7 is a key square — adjacent to e7. Taking f7 would restrict Black king.\n\nAfter g6→g7, White king on g7, Black king on e7: Black can still go to d8, d7, d6, e6, f6, f7, f8 — but f7 is now attacked by White king? No: g7 attacks f7 (diagonally left-down), yes! So after g6→g7, f7 is controlled by White.\n\nThus, if Black king tries to go to f7, it would be adjacent to White king — invalid. So Black cannot go to f7.\n\nSimilarly, g7 controls f6? No: from g7 to f6 is one diagonal down-left — yes, so f6 is also attacked.\n\nSo after K: g6-g7, White king on g7 attacks:\n- f7 (diagonally), h7, f6, h6, f8, h8, g6, g8\n\nIn particular, f6 and f7 are both under control.\n\nNow, Black king on e7 wants to go to f6 or f7 — but both are attacked. So those moves become unavailable.\n\nCurrent legal moves for Black after White plays g6→g7:\n- d8 (safe)\n- d7 (e7→d7: adjacent to e6, f7? d7 is file 4, rank 7; g7 is file 7, rank 7 — not adjacent. d7 is not attacked by g7. OK)\n- d6 (file 4, rank 6 — too far)\n- e6 (file 5, rank 6 — adjacent to f6, which is attacked, but e6 itself not attacked unless White controls it. g7 does not attack e6 — too far)\n- f6 — attacked by g7 → illegal\n- f7 — attacked by g7 → illegal\n- f8 — file 6, rank 8 → g7 attacks f8? g7 to f8 is one diagonal up-left: yes → f8 is attacked → illegal\n- d8 — safe\n\nSo Black loses access to f6, f7, f8 — three critical escape squares.\n\nOnly available: d8, d7, d6, e6.\n\nMuch more restricted.\n\nCompare with other moves — none of them attack any squares near e7.\n\nFor example, g6→f5: f5 attacks e4, e5, e6, f4, f6, g4, g5, g6 — so e6 and f6 are attacked.\n\nSo after f5, Black cannot go to e6 or f6.\n\nBut still allows f7 and f8.\n\nSo loss of two squares vs g7’s three squares.\n\nAlso, g7 is higher rank — better for controlling back ranks.\n\nMoreover, g7 prepares potential march to f8 or f6 later.\n\nTherefore, **K: g6-g7** is the strongest move — maximizes restriction of Black king.\n\nReason: Moving to g7 cuts off three key flight squares (f6, f7, f8) for the Black king, significantly reducing its mobility and improving White’s chances of driving it to the edge. It centralizes on the 7th rank and sets up for gaining opposition or zugzwang later.\n\nAnswer: K: g6-g7"}
