nk 7)\n- g7 is file g (3 away), rank 7 → distance is |g-d|=3 files, same rank → not adjacent. So g7 is **not adjacent** to d7.\n\nAdjacent squares to d7 are: c6, c7, c8, d6, d8, e6, e7, e8.\n\ng7 is three files away — so **f6-g7 is safe**.\n\nBut let\'s compute distance between g7 and d7: files differ by 3 (g - d = 3), ranks same → minimum king move is 3 moves apart → no issue.\n\nSo all listed moves are likely legal.\n\nBut let\'s evaluate each:\n\n- K: f6-e5 — Retreats slightly, loses ground. Not ideal.\n- K: f6-f5 — Downward, passive.\n- K: f6-f7 — Moves to f7, same rank as Black king, but closer. Controls e7, g7, etc.\n- K: f6-g5 — Sideways, not central.\n- K: f6-g6 — Good: advances to g6, controls f7, h7, f6, h6, g7 — supports potential mating net if other pieces existed. Cuts off escape.\n- K: f6-g7 — Moves to g7, which is on same rank as Black king. From g7, White king is close to centralizing and can head toward queenside.\n\nBut best plan is to **oppose** or **approach** the Black king to reduce its mobility.\n\nFrom f6, target is to drive Black king toward edge.\n\nBlack king on d7 — ideal for White king to occupy d5, c5, etc., but currently at f6.\n\nSo best move is to **centralize and approach**.\n\nBest candidate: **K: f6-e5** — heads toward d6/c6, cuts off central escape.\n\nWait — better: **K: f6-f7** — now White king on f7, Black on d7 — both on 7th rank. This establishes "opposition" potential.\n\nBut f7 is adjacent to e7 and g7, not directly threatening.\n\nAlternatively, **K: f6-e6** — but that move is not listed.\n\nAvailable moves do not include e6.\n\nListed: [K: f6-e5, K: f6-f5, K: f6-f7, K: f6-g5, K: f6-g6, K: f6-g7]\n\nSo among these:\n\n- **K: f6-g6** — goes to g6, which is a strong central outpost. From g6, White king can go to f7, e6, etc. It\'s a solid step forward.\n\n- **K: f6-f7** — also good, same rank advancement.\n\nBut **f7** is closer to the Black king’s flank? No, d7 to f7 is two files apart — knight move apart.\n\nBut from f7, White can go to e7 next, which is adjacent to d7 — forcing Black to move.\n\nSimilarly, from g6, path to e7 is longer.\n\nSo **K: f6-f7** is better — it gains space on the 7th rank and threatens to penetrate further.\n\nAlso, **K: f6-e5** is backward.\n\nBest: **K: f6-g7** — wait, g7?\n\ng7 is even farther from d7 than f7.\n\nd7 to g7 is three files apart.\n\nBut from f7, White king is on f7, Black on d7 — distance: |f-d|=2, same rank.\n\nThen White can play e7 next, attacking d7 directly.\n\nYes — **f6-f7** is excellent.\n\nAfter f6-f7, Black must respond, and then White can advance to e7 or g7 depending on reply.\n\nAlternatively, **K: f6-e5** allows Ke6 later, but that\'s slower.\n\nSince we want fastest approach, **f6-f7** is direct.\n\nAlso, **K: f6-g6** is okay, but less central.\n\nBest: **K: f6-f7**\n\nReason: It advances the White king to the 7th rank, maintaining proximity to the center and preparing to occupy e7 or g7 next, cutting off the Black king’s movement and aiming to gain opposition. This increases winning chances in a king-and-pawn endgame context (though no pawns exist yet, it\'s ideal positioning). Among the valid options, f7 is the most active square.\n\nAnswer: K: f6-f7'}
