er.\n\nK: d7-c8 cuts off retreat toward a8 and begins boxing the Black King toward the a-file. It also avoids symmetry and prepares to potentially support a future zugzwang if more pieces were present (though irrelevant here). Still, among options, it exerts the most relative pressure.\n\nBut actually, **K: d7-d6** places the king one step closer to the center of the board and reduces the Black King’s mobility most effectively. From d6, White can respond flexibly to any Black king move, aiming to approach and eventually confine it.\n\nWait — current position: White King on d7, Black King on b6. Distance is critical. The optimal plan is to centralize and reduce the opposition.\n\nFrom d7, moving to d6 puts the White King directly in front of the Black King along the same rank file group. This is progress.\n\nActually, **K: d7-c7** would be ideal, but it's not listed — so not available.\n\nAmong the given:\n\n- d7-d6 brings the king to e5-type centralization path.\n- But let's compute opposition: current files differ by 2 (d vs b), ranks differ by 1 (7 vs 6). Not direct opposition.\n\nMoving to **d7-c8** goes to c8, which is adjacent to b8 and b7 — helps control escape routes.\n\nBut best practical choice: **K: d7-d6** — centralizing, reducing space, preparing to chase.\n\nWait — valid moves include **K: d7-e6**, **K: d7-d6**, etc.\n\nLet’s assess proximity:\n\n- From d7 to b6: Δfile = 2, Δrank = 1 → Chebyshev distance = max(2,1) = 2\n- Goal: minimize distance.\n\nAfter **K: d7-d6**: now on d6, distance to b6 is max(2,0) = 2 — same.\n\nAfter **K: d7-c7** — not allowed.\n\nAfter **K: d7-c8**: now c8, distance to b6: max(|c-b|=1, |8-6|=2) → 2\n\nSame.\n\nBut **K: d7-e7**: e7 to b6 → Δf=3, Δr=1 → dist=3 — worse.\n\n**K: d7-d8**: d8 to b6 → Δf=2, Δr=2 → dist=2\n\nAll similar.\n\nBut **K: d7-c7** not available.\n\nWait — looking again: the Black King is on b6, White on d7.\n\nIf White plays **K: d7-c7**, that would be ideal — but it's not in the list. So invalid or not generated.\n\nAvailable: d7-c8, d7-d6, d7-d8, d7-e6, d7-e7, d7-e8.\n\nNow, **K: d7-c8** — moves to c8. Now, c8 is adjacent to b8 and b7 — both near b6. This starts boxing Black in the a/b-file area.\n\nFrom c8, next move could be c7 or b7, approaching.\n\nWhereas **d7-d6** goes to d6 — further in terms of cutting off queenside.\n\nAlso, after **d7-c8**, if Black plays Kb6-a6, then Kc8-b8 wins opposition.\n\nSimilarly, if Kb6-c6, then Kc8-c7 maintains pressure.\n\nSo **K: d7-c8** is more effective at taking the distant opposition and driving the Black King toward the edge.\n\nThus, best move is **K: d7-c8**\n\nReason: It begins to restrict the Black King’s mobility by taking a key square on the c-file and preparing to gain opposition or drive the king toward the a-file, maximizing chances to win if Black makes an error.\n\nAnswer: K: d7-c8"}
